{% extends "tutorial_base.html.j2" %}

{% block tutorial_body %}
        <!-- Intro Card -->
        <div class="doc-intro-card">
          <h2>From Multi-Tube CAD Weldment to Multi-Stick G-Code</h2>
          <p>
            Roll cages, chassis frames, handrails, and headache racks pack dozens of tube sizes into one STEP file. Load your assembly once to sort parts by cross-section and filter only what fits the stock in your chuck. Adjacent miters and fishmouth copes flip and roll to interlock end-to-end, optional common-line cuts merge matching ends, and every stick cuts from the free end back toward the chuck so the tube stays rigid.
          </p>
          <div class="doc-intro-grid">
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Profile Grouping</span>
              <span class="value">Auto Cross-Section Match</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Packing Algorithm</span>
              <span class="value">Interlocking 1D + Common-Line</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-intro-stat">
              <span class="label">Cut Sequencing</span>
              <span class="value">Free-End &rarr; Chuck (Rigid Support)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 1
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 01</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Load the <code class="mono">Headache Rack [COMPLEX]</code> Multi-Body Fixture</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Open the left sidebar's <strong>Quick CAD Fixtures</strong> panel, or drop in your own multi-body <code class="mono">.step</code> file.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip">Headache Rack [COMPLEX]</span> in <strong>Quick CAD Fixtures</strong>.</li>
              <li>All <strong>37 solids</strong> load in seconds with their cross-section sizes, lengths, and cut features ready.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 1: Clicking 'Headache Rack [COMPLEX]' in the Quick CAD Fixtures panel</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/nesting-1-fixture.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/nesting-1-fixture.png" alt="Step 1: Selecting Headache Rack [COMPLEX] in Quick CAD Fixtures" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 2
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 02</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Switch to the <code class="mono">Multi-Part Pack Nesting (37 PARTS)</code> Workspace</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Your full 37-part rack appears in the top-left <strong>Entire Model Overview</strong>. Switch to nesting to lay parts onto raw sticks:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click the <span class="ui-chip">Multi-Part Pack Nesting (37 PARTS)</span> tab at the top of the workspace, <em>or</em></li>
              <li>Click the blue <span class="ui-chip cyan">Open Pack</span> button in the <strong>Multi-Part Pack Nesting</strong> card on the left sidebar.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 2: Opening the Multi-Part Pack Nesting workspace (37 detected parts)</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/nesting-2-open-pack.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/nesting-2-open-pack.png" alt="Step 2: Clicking the Multi-Part Pack Nesting (37 PARTS) tab or Open Pack button" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 3
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 03</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Open the <code class="mono">CUTTING PROFILE</code> Cross-Section Selector</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              One STEP file often mixes tube sizes, such as <code class="mono">38.1 × 38.1 mm</code> posts, <code class="mono">25.4 × 25.4 mm</code> louvers, and <code class="mono">OD 14.3 mm</code> round bars. Parts group by cross-section so only matching profiles pack onto the same stick.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip">CUTTING PROFILE</span> in the nesting header bar (showing <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1x38.1mm) (7 parts)</code>) to switch tube sizes right inside the workspace.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 3: Clicking the CUTTING PROFILE dropdown to choose which stock size to nest</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/nesting-3-profile-selector.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/nesting-3-profile-selector.png" alt="Step 3: Highlighting the CUTTING PROFILE selector bar in Multi-Part Pack Nesting" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 4
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 04</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Select <code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)</code></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              The dropdown lists every tube profile found in your CAD assembly and its exact part count:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Rect (38.1x38.1mm) (7 parts)</code>: 1.5" × 1.5" square HSS frame tubes.</li>
              <li><span class="ui-chip">Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)</span>: 1.0" × 1.0" square HSS crossbars and louvers.</li>
              <li><code class="mono">Profile_HSS_Round (OD 14.3mm) (2 parts)</code>: round tube inserts.</li>
              <li>Select <span class="ui-chip">Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)</span> to filter the table and select all 17 matching parts.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 4: Selecting 'Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)' from the detected profiles</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/nesting-4-select-profile.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/nesting-4-select-profile.png" alt="Step 4: Dropdown showing detected tube profiles and selecting Profile_HSS_Rect (25.4x25.4mm) (17 parts)" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 5
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 05</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Inspect Multi-Stick Packing (<code class="mono">99.8% Efficiency</code>) &amp; Click <code class="mono">⚡ Compile Pack G-Code</code></h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              All <strong>17 pieces</strong> (<code class="mono">7480.3 mm</code> total cut length) pack onto <strong>2 sticks</strong> of <code class="mono">6000 mm</code> stock with a <code class="mono">10.0 mm</code> part gap:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Stick 1 of 2 Preview Bar:</strong> Fits 7 parts (<code class="mono">Part 10</code>, <code class="mono">Part 9</code>, <code class="mono">Part 34</code>, <code class="mono">Part 36</code>, <code class="mono">Part 4</code>, <code class="mono">Part 24</code>, <code class="mono">Part 35</code>) totaling <code class="mono">5987 mm / 6000 mm</code>. Only <code class="mono">13 mm</code> remains (<span class="ui-chip emerald">99.8% EFFICIENCY</span>). Use <code class="mono">◀ Prev Stick</code> / <code class="mono">Next Stick ▶</code> to inspect Stick 2.</li>
              <li><strong>Interlocking Miters &amp; Copes (180&deg; Flip + A-Axis Roll):</strong> Every tube is tested with 180&deg; end-for-end flips and A-axis rotations. Matching miters and fishmouth copes nest face-to-face instead of leaving triangular scrap wedges.</li>
              <li><strong>Common-Line Sever Cuts:</strong> Check <strong>Common-line sever cuts</strong> to merge matching 90&deg; cutoffs or complementary miters into one shared on-kerf pierce. You cut cutoff pierces in half and leave zero scrap rings on the table.</li>
              <li><strong>Chuck Jaw &ldquo;No-Cut&rdquo; Dead Zone &amp; Min Stock to Load:</strong> Your machine's <strong>Chuck Jaw Dead Zone</strong> (default <code class="mono">65 mm</code>) subtracts from stick length (<code class="mono">L_available = L_stick - L_dead</code>). A red hatched no-cut zone marks the chuck end of the stick bar, 3D chuck jaws show in the simulator, and <strong>Min Stock to Load</strong> (<code class="mono">L_cut + L_dead</code>) keeps your torch clear of the jaws.</li>
              <li><strong>Auto-Scribe Piece IDs (<code class="mono">P1</code>, <code class="mono">P2</code>) &amp; Mating Callouts (<code class="mono">P1</code> &harr; <code class="mono">P12</code>):</strong> Multi-part assemblies assign short IDs (<code class="mono">P1</code>, <code class="mono">P2</code>, &hellip;, <code class="mono">P12</code>) matching your part list. Turn on <strong>Auto-Scribe Part IDs</strong> to mark stick-font labels with EasyScribe (<code class="mono">#1114=3</code>) or Plasma Mark (<code class="mono">#1114=2</code> at <code class="mono">15A</code>).
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Part IDs &amp; Witness Marks:</strong> Each part gets its own ID plus weld witness footprints where branch tubes or brackets touch a header (<code class="mono">&le; 0.5 mm</code>).</li>
                  <li><strong>Mating Joint Callouts:</strong> The mating ID scribes inside each end cut (e.g., <code class="mono">P12</code> on <code class="mono">P1</code>) and beside the receiving witness mark (e.g., <code class="mono">P1</code> on <code class="mono">P12</code>) for fast weld fit-up.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li><strong>1D Rotary Stick Strip Controls:</strong> Reorder packed tubes along the stick, flip parts <code class="mono">180&deg;</code>, nudge rotary phase <code class="mono">&Delta;A</code>, and adjust per-part gaps right inside the Pack panel.</li>
              <li><strong>Synced Cut-Path &amp; Marking Settings:</strong> Edits to cut-path or marking settings sync between Single-Part and Nesting views.</li>
              <li><strong>Quantities, Spacing &amp; Table Controls:</strong> Adjust <strong>Part Spacing (Gap)</strong> (<code class="mono">10 mm</code>), <strong>Stock Tube Length</strong> (<code class="mono">6000 mm</code>), <strong>Auto-tab pack seams</strong>, and part quantities with gloved-friendly <code class="mono">24 px</code> checkboxes and <code class="mono">+</code>/<code class="mono">-</code> buttons.</li>
              <li>Click <span class="ui-chip emerald">⚡ Compile Pack G-Code</span> at the top of the nesting panel to build your multi-stick 4-axis programs.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 5: 17 parts packed across 2 sticks (99.8% efficiency on Stick 1) and clicking Compile Pack G-Code</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/nesting-5-compile.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/nesting-5-compile.png" alt="Step 5: Multi-Stick arrangement bar showing 99.8% efficiency and Compile Pack G-Code button" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 6
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="step-6">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 06</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Simulate Stick 1 / Stick 2 in 3D &amp; Download Per-Stick <code class="mono">.nc</code> Programs</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              After compiling, the <strong>3D Cut Simulation</strong> opens and the right-hand <strong>G-Code Post-Processor Terminal</strong> creates one tab per stick:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Switch &amp; Download Stick G-Code (Top-Right Panel):</strong> Pick <span class="ui-chip">Stick 1: pack_stick_1.nc</span> or <span class="ui-chip">Stick 2: pack_stick_2.nc</span>, then click <span class="ui-chip emerald">⬇ Download pack_stick_1.nc</span>.</li>
              <li><strong>Multi-Stick 3D Simulation (Center Viewport):</strong> Use <span class="ui-chip cyan">◀ Stick 1 of 2 ▶</span> and click <span class="ui-chip">▶ Play</span> to verify the full 6000 mm cut order from the free end (<code class="mono">X = -5991.78 mm</code>) back toward the chuck (<code class="mono">X = 0 mm</code>).</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="doc-callout tip">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">✓ Why Cuts Run Free-End to Chuck</div>
              <p>
                On a 20ft / 6000 mm tube held in one chuck, cuts start at the farthest free end (<code class="mono">Piece 1/7</code>) and move toward the chuck. Each part drops off only after all its holes and end miters are cut, keeping the remaining stock rigidly supported until the last cut.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">📦 Download All Sticks in One Archive (.zip)</div>
              <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                For multi-stick jobs (<code class="mono">pack_stick_1.nc</code>, <code class="mono">pack_stick_2.nc</code>, <code class="mono">pack_stick_3.nc</code>, etc.), click <strong>Download All Sticks (.zip)</strong> to grab every stick file together. Copy the <code class="mono">.zip</code> straight to your USB drive so you never mix up stock runs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="doc-figure">
            <div class="doc-figure-bar">
              <span class="doc-figure-caption"><span class="dot"></span> Step 6: Selecting Stick 1 / Stick 2 tabs and downloading pack_stick_1.nc</span>
              <span class="doc-figure-zoom">🔍 Click image to enlarge</span>
            </div>
            <div class="doc-figure-img-wrap">
              <picture>
                <source srcset="assets/nesting-6-download.webp" type="image/webp" />
                <img src="assets/nesting-6-download.png" alt="Step 6: Downloading pack_stick_1.nc and switching sticks in 3D Machine Simulation" width="2324" height="1397" loading="lazy" />
              </picture>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================================================================
             FLAT-PLATE PACK MODE PRIMITIVES & REMNANT GUILLOTINE CROP CUT
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="flat-sheet-pack-primitives">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge" style="background: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.15); color: var(--cyan-400); border-color: rgba(6, 182, 212, 0.35);">FLAT PACK</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">2D Flat-Sheet Pack Options &amp; Remnant Guillotine Crop Cut</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              Switch Nesting to 2D flat plates (<code class="mono">.dxf</code>, <code class="mono">.svg</code>, or <code class="mono">.flat</code> STEP sheets) for flat-sheet pack controls. Inactive Crop and Chain options stay hidden until you enable them.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>Open-Path Left / Right / Center Kerf (<code class="mono">Maker</code> &amp; <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>):</strong> Pick <span class="ui-chip">Center (On-Line)</span>, <span class="ui-chip">Left</span>, or <span class="ui-chip">Right</span> kerf offset on open slits, edge trims, and bend reliefs. Offset direction follows your CAD stroke direction.</li>
              <li><strong>Thick-Plate Edge-Start Piercing (<code class="mono">Maker</code> &amp; <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>):</strong> Enable <span class="ui-chip">Edge-Start (Thick Plate)</span> (default overhang <code class="mono">4.0 mm</code>) on heavy plate (<code class="mono">&ge; 12 mm</code> / <code class="mono">1/2&Prime;</code>). Your torch probes IHS on steel at the start point, retracts to pierce height, shifts off-edge into open air to fire, and locks THC until <code class="mono">6.0 mm</code> onto solid steel.</li>
              <li><strong>Flared Chain / Bridge Cutting (<code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>):</strong> Link adjacent parts with <span class="ui-chip">Daisy-Chain (Single Pierce)</span> using flared bridges that skip half-shape retracing, or use <span class="ui-chip">Micro-Bridge (Connected)</span> (default bridge width <code class="mono">3.0 mm</code> or stock auto-default, max link distance <code class="mono">25.0 mm</code>) for retention tabs. Interior holes always cut before chained contours, obstacle-crossing links are skipped, and Chain/Bridge cutting stays separate from Common-Line cutting.</li>
              <li><strong>2D Interactive Sheet Drag, Rotate, Flip &amp; Collision Halos:</strong> Drag parts in <code class="mono">(&Delta;X, &Delta;Y)</code>, rotate in <code class="mono">15&deg;</code> snaps or free angles, and flip H/V. Live halos flag part-spacing and sheet-edge collisions, and <span class="ui-chip">Reset Auto-Nest</span> restores the auto-pack anytime (<code class="mono">Maker</code> auto-nests 10 flat / 5 rotary with unlimited manual placement; <code class="mono">Pro Shop</code> unlimited auto-nest).</li>
              <li><strong>Void THC Auto-Lock &amp; Flat Corner Loops:</strong> Void lock holds THC off within <code class="mono">0.5 &times; kerf + 4.0 mm</code> of prior kerf slots and pierce craters so the torch never dives across earlier cuts. On <code class="mono">.flat</code> profiles, set your corner loop radius (default <code class="mono">3.0 mm</code>) for sharp square corners.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #fff; margin-top: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
              Tutorial: Cropping &amp; Saving a Clean Sheet Remnant (<code class="mono">Pro Shop</code>)
            </h3>
            <p>
              Use <strong>Guillotine Crop Cut</strong> in Pack Mode to square off leftover sheet drop after your parts finish cutting:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li><strong>1. Nest parts &amp; open 2D sheet controls:</strong> Arrange parts on a flat sheet in <strong>Nesting</strong> and find the docked 2D sheet controls next to the 2D Pack Part Preview.</li>
              <li><strong>2. Pick crop mode &amp; margin:</strong> Choose <code class="mono">Best Single Cut (Max Remnant)</code>, <code class="mono">L-Cut (Both X &amp; Y)</code>, <code class="mono">Vertical (X)</code>, or <code class="mono">Horizontal (Y)</code> in <span class="ui-chip">Guillotine Crop Cut</span>. Margin inputs appear automatically (default <code class="mono">kerf + 5.0 mm</code>).</li>
              <li><strong>3. Check the 2D Pack Preview SVG:</strong> Verify the dashed <code class="mono">CROP</code> line(s), off-sheet entry marker, and highlighted <code class="mono">Usable Remnant</code> rectangle before compiling.</li>
              <li><strong>4. Cut parts first, then crop:</strong> All part contours run first, followed by the off-edge guillotine crop with void-crossing THC lock. See <a href="dxf-svg-flat-sheet.html#tutorial-remnant-crop" style="color: var(--amber-400);">Tutorial 10: Flat-Sheet CAM &amp; Remnant Crop</a> for the full workflow.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
